      * ZA0510 - allocation priority lookup and summary helper. Legacy system
      * (Part 5), lesson 05-04 (arrays / tables / DS / error handling /
      * QCMDEXC). Standalone teaching program per design.objects[4].purpose:
      * NOT wired into the JU0900C -> ZA0500 chain.
      *
      * NOT YET COMPILED ON REAL HARDWARE (this session is out of its SSH
      * connection-count budget). Column positions come only from
      * tools/gen/rpg3.mjs and, where noted below, from RPG/400 Reference
      * (work/design/refs/rpg400ref.txt) by inference - not from a real
      * CRTRPGPGM. Verify with CRTRPGPGM before relying on this source.
      *
      * 1. /COPY placement (FLDREFR).
      *    Per fldrefr.rpg's own header (the authoritative spec for this),
      *    FLDREFR is copied in with copyDirective('I', 'FLDREFR'), placed
      *    in the I-spec area AFTER this program's own record-level I-specs
      *    (the ZAIKOR override block below) and BEFORE the C-specs. RPG/400
      *    Reference "Figure 1. Order of the Types of Specifications" (lines
      *    890-904) requires H-F-E-L-I-C-O source order; data-structure specs
      *    "as a whole must come after all record-level I-specs" (rpg3.mjs
      *    iSpecDS() comment) - so FLDREFR's copied-in CMNFLD DS, and this
      *    program's own CMDDS/PSDS/ZAINF/PRIDS data structures, all sit
      *    after the ZAIKOR block and before the first C-spec.
      *
      *    IMPORTANT: CMNFLD (copied in from FLDREFR) defines subfields named
      *    ZASHO and ZASU (src/legacy/qrpgsrc/fldrefr.rpg) - the exact same
      *    names as ZAIKOM's own external fields ZASHO/ZASU (db/v1/zaikom.pf),
      *    and they match field-for-field: both ZASHO are 6A, and both ZASU
      *    are numeric, length 7, 0 decimal positions (db/v1/zaikom.pf "7S 0"
      *    zoned decimal; fldrefr.rpg's CMNFLD subfield has no format letter
      *    and 0 decimals, i.e. zoned too). RPG/400 Reference's Field Names
      *    rule (p.6-7) is not a flat uniqueness ban for a matching pair like
      *    this: "A field name can appear on more than one field definition
      *    statement if each definition using that name has the same data
      *    type, the same length, and the same number of decimal positions.
      *    All definitions using the same name refer to a single field (that
      *    is, the same area in storage)." Since ZASHO/ZASU's type/length/
      *    decimals already match between ZAIKOM and CMNFLD, reusing the
      *    names as-is would compile fine - but it would NOT give two
      *    independent values: it would make ZAIKOM's own per-record ZASHO/
      *    ZASU and CMNFLD's common-field ZASHO/ZASU the SAME storage area,
      *    so whichever one is assigned last would silently overwrite the
      *    other's value, even though they mean different things (this
      *    record's own product code/quantity vs. FLDREFR's general-purpose
      *    common fields). This program renames ZAIKOM's fields via I-spec
      *    field override (cols 21-30 external name -> cols 53-58 program
      *    name, RPG/400 Reference p.149/153, same mechanism tk0100.rpg uses
      *    for TANTOM's TANTOCODE/TANTONAME - itself flagged there as
      *    reference-based/not yet compiled for this specific rename form) to
      *    ZKSHO/ZKSU before FLDREFR is copied in, so the two meanings get
      *    two separate names/storage areas instead of colliding into one.
      *
      * 2. Compile-time TABLE pair (product code -> priority), not a single
      *    alternating-format table pair - and where its "**" data actually
      *    goes in the source.
      *    RPG/400 Reference "Positions 46-57 (Second Array Description)"
      *    (lines 8358-8374) lets ONE E-spec line describe two tables loaded
      *    in alternating format on the SAME input record. rpg3.mjs's eSpec()
      *    does not implement cols 46-57 (only cols 27-45, one array/table per
      *    call - see its own header comment). Per this lesson's design brief,
      *    that gap is not worked around by hand-deriving those columns;
      *    instead this program declares TWO INDEPENDENT compile-time tables,
      *    TABPCD (product code) and TABPRI (priority), each its own "**"
      *    data block. This is not an approximation: RPG/400 Reference's own
      *    "LOKUP with Two Tables" section (lines 22619-22677, Figure 113/114,
      *    TABEMP/TABPAY) documents exactly this - two separately-declared
      *    tables with the same entry count, one searched by LOKUP, the
      *    other's corresponding element retrieved via the LOKUP result field
      *    - as the standard way to look up a related pair of tables.
      *
      *    Placement of the "**" data: Figure 1 ("Order of the Types of
      *    Specifications", lines 889-904) draws the required source order as
      *    Control/File/Extension/Line-Counter/Input/Calculation/Output, THEN
      *    "Compile-Time Array and Table Data" (then alternate collating
      *    sequence / file translation records, unused here) - i.e. ALL
      *    compile-time array/table data comes after every H/F/E/I/C/O spec,
      *    not right after each table's own E-spec header. "Loading a
      *    Compile-Time Array" (lines 21934-21941) confirms this directly:
      *    "enter array input data into records ... following the source
      *    records for this program". Both "**" blocks below therefore sit
      *    after the last O-spec, in E-spec declaration order (TABPCD then
      *    TABPRI - "arrays are loaded in the order in which they are
      *    described in the extension specifications", line 22008-22009).
      *    (verify/part05-gen-probe/src/t0eds.rpg puts its own "**" data
      *    between its C-specs and O-specs; ju0300.rpg's header already notes
      *    that probe is itself not yet compiled, and Figure 1 above is a
      *    clearer, more direct source for this specific question, so this
      *    program follows Figure 1 rather than that probe's placement.)
      *
      *    Product codes are the real ones from db/v1/zaikom.pf / db/data/
      *    load_v1.sql (P00001-P00006). Priority values (1=highest .. 3=
      *    lowest) are invented teaching data, not a real business rule:
      *    P00001/P00002=1, P00003/P00004=2, P00005/P00006=3. Compile-time
      *    table data format for TABPRI (zoned decimal, length 1, 0 decimal
      *    positions) is written as a plain digit character per RPG/400
      *    Reference's zoned-decimal literal convention; this specific 1-
      *    digit unsigned case has no real-compile record in docs/probes.md.
      *
      * 3. LOKUP: TABPCD searched (factor 2), TABPRI is the related table
      *    named in the result field (RPG/400 Reference p.286, "LOKUP with
      *    Two Tables" p.401-402). Only an EQUAL search is needed (exact
      *    product-code match), so the resulting indicator goes in the EQ
      *    position (58-59) per the general LOKUP rule (p.286); no A/D
      *    sequence entry is coded on either E-spec, matching "if only an
      *    equal indicator is used, LOKUP searches the entire table" (p.287).
      *    Per p.286 ("No resulting indicator is set on if the search is not
      *    successful"), the EQ indicator is NOT cleared by an unsuccessful
      *    LOKUP, so it is SETOF before every LOKUP - same reasoning as
      *    za0500.rpg's SETOF 9091 before its CHAIN.
      *
      * 4. Summary array SQ + cross-foot, same technique as ju0300.rpg's CT:
      *    a RUN-TIME array (E-spec with entries-per-record left blank, no
      *    "**" data), one element per priority (1-3), accumulating ZAIKOM
      *    quantity (renamed ZKSU) as each record is read. GQTY is an
      *    independent running total (added once per record, not through the
      *    array); XFOOT sums SQ into XQTY at LR and XQTY is COMPed against
      *    GQTY as an integrity check (indicator 93 = match), printed as OK /
      *    MISMATCH - the same cross-footing pattern ju0300.rpg uses for XTOT
      *    vs GCNT.
      *
      *    Array-element result-field naming: RPG/400 Reference p.397 ("if an
      *    array element is to be used as a result field, the array name with
      *    the comma and index cannot exceed 6 characters" - this is just the
      *    physical 6-column width of the C-spec Result field, cols 43-48,
      *    restated). "SQ,PRI" is exactly 6 characters (array name SQ + comma
      *    + index field PRI), which is why the array is named SQ rather than
      *    something longer/more descriptive.
      *
      * 5. DS PRIDS: a separate summary data structure (distinct from the
      *    copied-in CMNFLD), giving each SQ element an alias name (PQ1/PQ2/
      *    PQ3) per the design brief's own suggested "natural use". RPG III
      *    has no keyword (RPG IV's OVERLAY) to make a DS subfield share
      *    storage with an already-declared array element, and no such
      *    overlay technique is confirmed anywhere in this repo's sources or
      *    in rpg400ref.txt as searched this session - so PQ1/PQ2/PQ3 are
      *    populated by an explicit Z-ADD copy from SQ,1/SQ,2/SQ,3 at LR (no
      *    len/dec repeated on these Z-ADDs: PQ1-PQ3 are already defined by
      *    the I-spec subfields below, and RPG/400 Reference requires len/dec
      *    only on a result field's FIRST definition - the same rule
      *    ju0300.rpg's header note 6a already applies to L1CNT/L2CNT), not a
      *    storage overlay. The O-specs below print PQ1/PQ2/PQ3 (not SQ
      *    directly), so the DS is genuinely what drives the report output.
      *
      * 6. Error indicator at C-spec cols 56-57 (item 7 of the design brief):
      *    the CALL to QCMDEXC below carries its error indicator in the LO
      *    position (56-57) - RPG/400 Reference's CALL entry (p.216): "Any
      *    valid resulting indicator can be specified in positions 56 and 57
      *    to be set on for an error returned from the called program" (54-55
      *    must stay blank). This is the same "_ ER _" column convention used
      *    by ACQ (p.205) and OUT (p.317); tk0100.rpg's H1 (LOCK IN) is the
      *    same convention for a different opcode. Indicator 85 here.
      *
      * 7. Program status DS (PSDS) - and a real per-file INFDS, ZAINF.
      *    The design brief's item 7 calls this "INFDS (program status data
      *    structure)", but RPG/400 Reference reserves "INFDS" specifically
      *    for the FILE information data structure (p.25, "A file information
      *    data structure (INFDS) can be defined for EACH FILE..."; F-spec
      *    continuation line, cols 53=K/54-59=INFDS/60-65=name). The
      *    construct rpg3.mjs's iSpecDS(option:'S') builds is the separate
      *    "Program Status Data Structure" (p.42-43) - not file-specific,
      *    declared on an I-spec (option 'S' in col 18), and never called
      *    "INFDS" anywhere this session found in rpg400ref.txt. The design
      *    brief's own parenthetical Japanese gloss (program-state-data-
      *    structure) does describe this construct correctly even though its
      *    preceding English label does not; that is what is built here,
      *    named PSDS.
      *
      *    STATUS and a routine-name subfield (named RTN here) are declared
      *    at their predefined From/To byte positions from Table 8 (STATUS=
      *    11-15, ROUTINE=29-36) rather than via the keyword form (cols
      *    44-51, literally "STATUS"/"ROUTINE"): rpg3.mjs's iSpecSubfield()
      *    only implements numeric from/to, not the keyword token, and this
      *    script does not hand-derive columns outside the generator. PSDS is
      *    not read by any logic below (kept available for a human debugging
      *    a real compile/run).
      *
      *    A true per-file INFDS for ZAIKOM is also coded now, named ZAINF
      *    (I-spec DS below, next to PSDS). rpg3.mjs's fSpec() had no way to
      *    emit an F-spec continuation line, so this fix adds a companion
      *    generator function, fSpecCont(), that does: col 53=K, cols 54-59=
      *    INFDS, cols 60-65=ZAINF (p.25), emitted right after FZAIKOM's main
      *    F-spec line and before FQSYSPRT's - still inside the F-spec block,
      *    per p.25's "make the following entries on a continuation line ...
      *    for the file description specifications" and Figure 1's H-F-E-...
      *    source order. ZAINF's two subfields sit at the predefined Table 1
      *    byte offsets (p.28, "Contents of the File Feedback Information ...
      *    (INFDS)"): FSTAT = zoned 5,0 at 11-15 (status code), FRTN =
      *    character 8 at 22-29 (name of the RPG/400 routine in which the
      *    exception/error occurred). FSTAT/FRTN are deliberately NOT named
      *    STATUS/RTN (PSDS's own subfield names): by the Field Names rule
      *    quoted in note 1 above, giving PSDS's STATUS (zoned 5,0 at 11-15
      *    within PSDS) and INFDS's status field (also zoned 5,0 at 11-15,
      *    but within the different DS ZAINF) the same name would make RPG
      *    treat them as ONE shared storage area, silently aliasing two
      *    unrelated values (program status vs. file status). Like PSDS,
      *    ZAINF is not read by any logic below (kept available for a human
      *    debugging a real compile/run).
      *
      * 8. INFSR: a real per-file F-spec INFSR continuation line, naming the
      *    same *PSSR subroutine used as the error handler below.
      *    Also emitted via fSpecCont() (note 7), right after the INFDS
      *    continuation line: col 53=K, cols 54-59=INFSR, cols 60-65=*PSSR
      *    (RPG/400 Reference p.37: col 53=K, cols 54-59=INFSR, cols 60-65=
      *    subroutine name). p.37 is explicit that this is not a free-form
      *    label: "Factor 1 of the BEGSR operation and factor 2 of the EXSR
      *    operation must contain the name of the subroutine that receives
      *    control (same name as specified in positions 60 through 65 of the
      *    file description specifications continuation line)" - so cols
      *    60-65 here MUST equal the BEGSR factor 1 and EXSR factor 2 used by
      *    the *PSSR subroutine below (both *PSSR per note 10's starred
      *    reading), and are coded *PSSR accordingly. p.37's own
      *    prose sentence ("The subroutine name can be PSSR...") drops the
      *    leading "*" the same way note 10 already documents this OCR doing
      *    to *CANCL in this same p.37-38 passage (lines 3096/3108) - so that
      *    sentence is read here as *PSSR, not as evidence for an un-starred
      *    PSSR, consistent with note 10 rather than contradicting it. This
      *    INFSR entry explicitly routes ZAIKOM's file exception/errors to
      *    the same *PSSR subroutine used for the LOKUP-not-found case below,
      *    rather than relying only on the "no INFSR present" default-to-
      *    PSSR behavior.
      *
      *    Chapter 2's general exception/error dispatch (p.24, Figure 7 steps
      *    1-4, lines 2202-2214) is explicit that "the appropriate
      *    exception/error subroutine (INFSR or PSSR)" (step 3) receives
      *    control - a single combined check, and here the two are the same
      *    routine anyway, since ZAIKOM's own INFSR entry above explicitly
      *    names *PSSR. So *PSSR here also catches unhandled runtime
      *    exceptions while reading ZAIKOM (e.g. a decimal data error), not
      *    only the LOKUP-not-found case. The one
      *    confirmed exception to this (p.38, lines 3127-3129: "If a file
      *    exception/error occurs during the start or end of a program,
      *    control passes to the RPG/400 default error handler, and not to
      *    the user-written file exception/error subroutine (INFSR)") is
      *    OPEN/CLOSE-time failures, which bypass INFSR *and* PSSR alike -
      *    exactly what probes.md's 04-06 finding shows on real hardware
      *    ("*PSSR ... did not solve" an implicit-open failure). Coding an
      *    explicit INFDS/INFSR pair does not change this: p.38's OPEN/
      *    CLOSE-time rule bypasses INFSR/PSSR regardless of whether INFSR is
      *    coded, so that narrow case remains out of scope here.
      *
      *    The LOKUP-not-found case is not an RPG-raised exception at all
      *    (LOKUP just leaves its indicator off - p.286), so it is routed to
      *    *PSSR by an explicit EXSR, which p.38 confirms is valid ("the
      *    programmer can explicitly call the file exception/error subroutine
      *    by specifying the name of the subroutine in factor 2 of the EXSR
      *    operation"). With the shipped table data (TABPCD covering exactly
      *    ZAIKOM's six product codes), the not-found path is not expected to
      *    fire; it is included as the "fatal LOKUP miss" case the design
      *    brief asks for.
      *
      * 9. QCMDEXC is NOT documented anywhere in rpg400ref.txt (grepped this
      *    session for QCMDEXC - zero matches), and has no real-compile record
      *    in docs/probes.md; work/design/part05-legacy-design.json's own
      *    unverifiedGeneratorFeatures says the same. It is called here as an
      *    ordinary CALL to the system program QSYS/QCMDEXC (RPG/400
      *    Reference's CALL opcode, p.214-217 - CALL itself IS confirmed by
      *    primary source, only the specific target program QCMDEXC is not),
      *    with two PARMs, per general IBM i API documentation (external to
      *    this repo, UNCONFIRMED here): (1) the command string to run, (2) a
      *    packed(15,5) field holding that string's length - matching the
      *    design brief's "length 15 5" wording exactly.
      *
      *    The command text is built into CMDDS (a plain DS, C1-C9 subfields
      *    covering its 57 bytes) by explicit RUNTIME MOVEL operations in
      *    *PSSR, NOT by I-spec compile-time subfield initialization: RPG/400
      *    Reference's own PARM rule (p.318, lines 18453-18458) says the PARM
      *    result field "cannot contain ... a data structure with initialized
      *    subfields" - so a DS built via iSpecSubfield()'s init/initValue
      *    could not legally be PARMed at all. Each MOVEL literal chunk is
      *    <=7 raw characters, so even with one embedded quote doubled
      *    (apostrophes doubled per p.7-8's "O'CLOCK" -> 'O''CLOCK' rule, for
      *    the two literal quotes SNDPGMMSG's own MSG('...') syntax needs) its
      *    RPG-encoded form is at most 10 characters including the two
      *    delimiting quotes - fitting the C-spec Factor2 field's 10-column
      *    width (cols 33-42) exactly, narrower than the 22-column I-spec
      *    init-value field this session first (wrongly) planned around.
      *    Since every chunk equals its target subfield's length exactly, each
      *    MOVEL is an exact same-length character move (RPG/400 Reference
      *    p.302, rule 1.d) - no truncation or padding involved. CMDDS is then
      *    passed whole to PARM (a plain, non-multi-occurrence, non-*LDA data
      *    structure is a legal PARM result field; only the excluded forms
      *    listed above are not).
      *
      *    MSGTYPE(*INFO)/TOPGMQ(*EXT) (send a free-text message to this job's
      *    external message queue) is used instead of MSGTYPE(*ESCAPE) because
      *    docs/probes.md's CPD2489 finding is that *ESCAPE specifically needs
      *    a registered MSGID, not free MSG() text; that finding is from a CL
      *    SNDPGMMSG call, not one driven through QCMDEXC from RPG, so
      *    carrying it over here is inference, not a direct confirmation. No
      *    message file is created for this task (one RPG source file only),
      *    so a free-text-eligible MSGTYPE is used rather than a registered
      *    MSGID.
      *
      * 10. Leading "*" on PSSR/CANCL: this source's own OCR is self-
      *     contradictory, so this program follows the majority reading.
      *     The design brief's ticket-3 note (part05-legacy-design.json) says
      *     "ENDSR *CANCL". Within rpg400ref.txt itself: BEGSR's own opcode
      *     entry (p.208, lines 13006-13008, "the keyword PSSR used in factor
      *     1"), the File Exception/Error Subroutine return-point list
      *     (p.18 lines 1840-1841, p.37-38 lines 3096/3108, "a literal with
      *     the value CANCL"/"CANCL Cancel the processing of the program"),
      *     and that same p.37 passage's INFSR prose ("The subroutine name
      *     can be PSSR...", see note 8 above) give these WITHOUT a leading
      *     star. But EXSR's own opcode entry (p.263, lines 15806-15807,
      *     "*PSSR used in factor 2 ... *INZSR used in factor 2") gives
      *     PSSR/INZSR WITH a star, consistently as a pair; Figure 4's
      *     program-cycle diagram (lines 1613-1650) also stars *DETC/*TOTC/
      *     *TOTL/*TERM/*CANCL throughout; and the index (lines 25116, 25638,
      *     25127, 26262, 26366) lists both *PSSR and *CANCL starred.
      *     tk0100.rpg's header already documents this same OCR text dropping
      *     leading "*" inconsistently on special words. No single reading is
      *     fully self-consistent within this document, so this program
      *     follows the more specific/more frequent starred reading (EXSR's
      *     own definition names *PSSR by exact, deliberate pairing with
      *     *INZSR; the diagram and index agree) - *PSSR in BEGSR factor 1,
      *     EXSR factor 2, AND the F-spec INFSR continuation line's cols
      *     60-65 (note 8; p.37's own matching rule requires all three to be
      *     the same literal name), and '*CANCL' in ENDSR factor 2 - rather
      *     than treating the design brief as wrong. This remains unverified
      *     against a real compile either way.
      *
      * 11. F-spec for QSYSPRT: za0500.rpg's O-specs reference QSYSPRT but
      *     that file has no FQSYSPRT F-spec line at all (checked its current
      *     source directly). ju0300.rpg does declare one correctly. This
      *     program follows ju0300.rpg's complete pattern, not za0500.rpg's.
      *
     H
     FZAIKOM  IP  E           K        DISK
     F                                              KINFDS ZAINF
     F                                              KINFSR *PSSR
     FQSYSPRT O   F     132            PRINTER
     E                    TABPCD  6   6  6
     E                    TABPRI  6   6  1 0
     E                    SQ          3  7 0
     IZAIKOR
     I              ZASHO                           ZKSHO
     I              ZASU                            ZKSU
     I/COPY FLDREFR
     ICMDDS       DS                             57
     I                                        1   7 C1
     I                                        8  14 C2
     I                                       15  21 C3
     I                                       22  28 C4
     I                                       29  35 C5
     I                                       36  42 C6
     I                                       43  49 C7
     I                                       50  56 C8
     I                                       57  57 C9
     IPSDS       SDS
     I                                       11  150STATUS
     I                                       29  36 RTN
     IZAINF       DS
     I                                       11  150FSTAT
     I                                       22  29 FRTN
     IPRIDS       DS                             21
     I                                        1   70PQ1
     I                                        8  140PQ2
     I                                       15  210PQ3
      * -- detail: one cycle per ZAIKOM record --
     C                     ADD  ZKSU      GQTY    90
     C                     SETOF                     80
     C           ZKSHO     LOKUPTABPCD    TABPRI         80
     C  N80                EXSR *PSSR
     C   80                Z-ADDTABPRI    PRI     10
     C   80                ADD  ZKSU      SQ,PRI
      * -- LR: cross-foot the array against the independent total --
     CLR                   XFOOTSQ        XQTY    90
     CLR         XQTY      COMP GQTY                     93
     CLR                   Z-ADDSQ,1      PQ1
     CLR                   Z-ADDSQ,2      PQ2
     CLR                   Z-ADDSQ,3      PQ3
     CLR                   EXCPT
      * -- subroutines --
      * *PSSR: fatal LOKUP-miss (explicit EXSR) and, program-wide,
      * RPG's default error/exception handler (see header note 8).
     CSR         *PSSR     BEGSR
      * build the QCMDEXC command text into CMDDS (see header note 9)
     C                     MOVEL'SNDPGMM' C1
     C                     MOVEL'SG MSG(' C2
     C                     MOVEL'''ZA0510'C3
     C                     MOVEL' ERROR'''C4
     C                     MOVEL') TOPGM' C5
     C                     MOVEL'Q(*EXT)' C6
     C                     MOVEL' MSGTYP' C7
     C                     MOVEL'E(*INFO' C8
     C                     MOVEL')'       C9
     C                     Z-ADD57        CMDLEN 155
     C                     CALL 'QCMDEXC'              85
     C                     PARM           CMDDS
     C                     PARM           CMDLEN
     CSR                   ENDSR'*CANCL'
     OQSYSPRT E
     O                         PQ1        9
     O                         PQ2       18
     O                         PQ3       27
     O                         GQTY      38
     O                         XQTY      49
     O                 93                55 'OK'
     O                N93                65 'MISMATCH'
**
P00001P00002P00003P00004P00005P00006
**
112233
